---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHero from "../../components/PageHero.astro";
import EvidenceDrawer from "../../components/EvidenceDrawer.astro";
import RrfExplorer from "../../islands/RrfExplorer";
---

<BaseLayout title="Reciprocal Rank Fusion" description="Step through Reciprocal Rank Fusion with every score term shown, change k, and compare it with NexusRAG's deployed gateway blend.">
  <PageHero eyebrow="Lab / ranking" title="Reciprocal Rank Fusion" lede="Two search systems disagree about which document is best. RRF merges them using only positions, so it never has to compare scores that were measured on different scales." />
  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container">
      <RrfExplorer client:visible />
      <noscript><p class="notice small">Editing needs JavaScript. The example above is computed at build time with the same function.</p></noscript>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container reading prose">
      <h2 class="h3">Reading the result</h2>
      <p>With the example rankings, D1 is near the top of both lists — second lexically, first semantically — so it wins. D2 is first lexically but missing from the semantic list, so it falls below D3, which sits in the middle of both lists. RRF rewards agreement between systems more than a single top position.</p>
      <p>The constant k decides how much rank position matters. With a small k the first place is worth much more than the second; with a large k positions count almost equally and documents found by more systems win. 60 is the value from the original paper and a common default.</p>
      <h2 class="h3">Rules this implementation follows</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Ranks start at 1; a document missing from a ranking contributes 0.</li>
        <li>k must be a finite number greater than 0; anything else is rejected, not clamped.</li>
        <li>A repeated id inside one ranking keeps its first (best) position and produces a warning.</li>
        <li>Equal scores are ordered by document id, so the result never depends on input order.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2 class="h3">How NexusRAG differs</h2>
      <p>NexusRAG's deployed gateway does not call a pure RRF function. It blends rank contributions with a dense weight α and adds a small bonus for lexical term coverage. Tick “Compare” above to see where the two orders agree and where they part.</p>
      <EvidenceDrawer claimIds={["nexus-fusion", "nexus-research-pipeline"]} summary="Evidence for the NexusRAG comparison" />
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
